  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Application of the right to freedom of association and collective bargaining strengthened</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Eswatini&#x2019;s application of the Freedom of Association and Protection of the Rights to Organize Convention, 1948 (No.87) and the Right to Organise and Collective Bargaining Convention, 1949 (No. 98) in Eswatini have come under the spotlight of the ILO&#x2019;s Supervisory bodies.  Government in collaboration with the Social Partners is to address concerns raised by the Standards Supervisory bodies due for submission in 2024. Government and the Social Partners are also considering recommendations of the SRM TWG for the ratification of up-to-date conventions, considering the abrogation or impending abrogation of outdated Conventions.  Within the context of reforms to the UNDS, and the centrality of the UNSDCF to the Decent Work Country Programme, the involvement of the Government of Eswatini and the Social Partners in the next UNSDCF process is critical to also ensure that the UNSDCF includes measures that address issues raised by the ILO supervisory bodies. 
The strategy of the intervention focuses on capacity and advisory support to the Government and the Social Partners towards the improved application of C87 and C98; increased capacity to report to the ILO supervisory bodies and to address issues raised by the ILO&#x2019;s supervisory; strengthened capacity by Government and the social partners to engage in social dialogue and collective bargaining; and increased awareness and sensitization of up-to-date Conventions, and the two OSH Core Conventions with a view to possible ratification.</narrative>
  </description>
